<h2>Measuring natural selection in the wild</h2>
<p>
  A major theme of the lab is how natural selection operates in the wild, and the
  mechanistic role it plays in shaping the phenotypes we observe. Since Fall 2015
  our long-term study on <a href="/research/lizard-island/">Lizard Island</a> in
  Miami has sampled every lizard on the island, between 800 and 1,000 at any given
  time, every spring and fall. Sampling twice a year lets us measure selection
  through the summer reproductive season and separately through the winter
  non-reproductive season, resolving variation that an annual census would be
  unable to recover.
</p>

<h2>From process to pattern</h2>
<p>
  The central ambition of the lab is bridging the process-pattern divide in ecology
  and evolution: collecting data in nature that explain large-scale patterns of
  diversity across space and through time.
</p>
